  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThe surface front has stalled out over northern IL at this point. \nThat and some upper level (500mb) vort maxes together will bring \nseveral more rounds of showers and potentially thunderstorms across \nthe state into the weekend. \n\nToday the rain is primarily near and south of the I-72 corridor. It \nis uncertain just how far much further north the rain will extend \nbut some scattered activity is not out of the question. There \nalready is some activity approaching the southwest CWA now (~07z) \nfrom the convection in central/northern MO, which will continue to \nspread east through the morning into the early afternoon. Severe \nweather and flash flooding are not expected at this time over the \nnext few days. Some areas that see multiple heavy showers/storms \nmove through could see some ponding or full ditches though. \n\nSeasonable temperatures are expected to last into Saturday. Then, \ntemperatures look to rise near or above 90 degrees for highs with \nincreasing humidity. The warmest days of the forecast being Sunday \ninto Tuesday (Monday being the hottest day). The LREF shows a 50-80% \nchance of apparent temperatures being greater than 95 degrees on \nMonday afternoon, south of I-74/Danville. However, Sunday through \nTuesday, multiple convective areas will move across northern and \ncentral IL. The heat indices and actual temperatures may not be able \nto achieve 90 degrees because of the additional rain/cloud cover. \nWednesday and Thursday next week looks to cool back off with highs \nin the upper 70s to low 80s.",
